<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Orungu Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1700 CE – 1927 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Myene-speaking kingdom of the Ogooue delta in Gabon that controlled the slave and later the rubber trade at Cape Lopez.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Orungu established a kingdom around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogooue">Ogooue</a> delta and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-lopez">Cape Lopez</a> by the early 18th century, displacing earlier inhabitants. Their position at the mouth of Gabon's largest river gave them control of trade between the interior and European ships.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Orungu king, with a council of clan heads, monopolised trade with Europeans at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-lopez">Cape Lopez</a>, acting as a middleman for slaves supplied from up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogooue">Ogooue</a>. In the 19th century Cape Lopez became an outlet for an illegal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, later replaced by trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebony">ebony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>French influence grew after the 1860s, and the area was absorbed into French <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gabon-french-colony">Gabon</a>. The kingship declined into a local chieftaincy, which the French ceased to recognise by the late 1920s.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1700 CE – 1927 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:93.53%;width:4.52%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1700 CE – 1927 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Myene religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Myene (Orungu)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/gabon">Gabon</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Orungu, Cape Lopez kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1814&amp;focus=orungu-kingdom" aria-label="Orungu Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5009 2194 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Orungu Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5262 2418h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5329 2450h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5329 2450h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Gabon. <a href="/atlas?year=1814&amp;focus=orungu-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

